{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory` is the JSSE provider-based factory for `TrustManager` objects. The documented lifecycle is to acquire it with a `getInstance` overload, initialize it with the initialization form appropriate to that factory, then call `getTrustManagers()`. Calling `getTrustManagers()` before initialization throws `IllegalStateException`.","The documented API event described by the finding is `TrustManagerFactory.init(KeyStore ks)`, specifically `init(null)`. The API documents the argument as “the key store, or null”; therefore null is a permitted API value, not an API-level error or prohibition.","The general `TrustManagerFactory` API does not guarantee what a null keystore means for every provider. Oracle's JSSE Reference Guide documents default truststore lookup for SunJSSE `PKIX` or `SunX509` trust-manager factories: it considers the `javax.net.ssl.trustStore` property and, when applicable, the `jssecacerts` and `cacerts` locations. Thus, default-truststore lookup is documented provider behavior rather than a provider-independent `TrustManagerFactory` contract.","Neither the official API nor the cited CrySL source uses the detector terms “whose loading was not observed” or `UnsatisfiedConstraint`. The official API does not define a static-analysis event for observing the loading of a default truststore.","Official documentation does not characterize `init(null)` as unsafe, deprecated, or incorrect and does not prescribe a remediation solely because null was passed. It documents alternatives for cases where an application or provider uses supplied trust material: `init(KeyStore)` with an appropriate `KeyStore`, or `init(ManagerFactoryParameters)` with the provider-appropriate parameter implementation. The JSSE guide advises selecting the initialization form appropriate to the factory and consulting the provider vendor if necessary.","For portable Java SE algorithm selection, the current Java SE standard-names specification requires support for the `PKIX` `TrustManagerFactory` algorithm. `SunX509` is documented as a SunJSSE algorithm, not as a Java SE cross-provider requirement.","`TrustManagerFactory` does not define a persisted truststore format. It accepts a `KeyStore` object or provider-specific `ManagerFactoryParameters`; persistence and formats are governed by `KeyStore` implementations and providers. The `KeyStore` API says persistence mechanisms are unspecified, and current Java SE requires support for the `PKCS12` keystore type.","The `TrustManagerFactory` API and the cited CrySL rule define no TLS wire format, enabled or negotiated protocol version, cipher-suite configuration, or TLS migration behavior. The official documentation provides no guarantee that an explicit `KeyStore` initialization is behaviorally equivalent to provider default-truststore resolution.","`TrustManagerFactory` has existed since Java 1.4. Java 8 documentation already specified that `init(KeyStore)` accepts “the key store, or null,” so null acceptance is not newly introduced behavior.","The supplied primary CrySL source declares its governed specification type verbatim as `SPEC javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory`. Its relevant matching declarations are verbatim:
```
EVENTS
	g1: getInstance(algorithm);
	g2: getInstance(algorithm, _);
	Get := g1 | g2;

	i1: init(keyStore);
	i2: init(params);
	Init := i1 | i2;

	gtm1: trustManager = getTrustManagers();
	GetTrustMng := gtm1;
```
It therefore declares `getInstance` with one argument and with two arguments (the second expressed as `_`), `init` with one `keyStore` or `params` argument, and zero-argument `getTrustManagers()` whose return is bound to `trustManager`. The associated declared object types are `java.lang.String` for `algorithm`, `java.security.KeyStore` for `keyStore`, `javax.net.ssl.ManagerFactoryParameters` for `params`, and `javax.net.ssl.TrustManager[]` for `trustManager`.","The supplied CrySL source's order clause is verbatim `Get, Init, GetTrustMng?`. Its sole `CONSTRAINTS` condition is verbatim `algorithm in {"PKIX", "SunX509"};`. It additionally declares these requirements verbatim: `generatedKeyStore[keyStore];` and `generatedManagerFactoryParameters[params];`. It declares no literal null predicate, default-truststore lookup predicate, or truststore-loading predicate.","Under the source's declared scope, the rule is for `javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory`, not a generic textual match for methods with the same names. Thus a method merely sharing `getInstance`, `init`, or `getTrustManagers` by name is not the API match declared by this rule. The CrySL file itself does not document an analyzer's operational type resolution, alias handling, reflection handling, or unresolved-call behavior; those implementation details cannot be established from this source alone."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official API documentation for the governed type, initialization lifecycle, `init(KeyStore)` null contract, and `getTrustManagers()` pre-initialization behavior.","limitations":"Defines the public provider-based API but does not define detector evidence such as `generatedKeyStore`, `UnsatisfiedConstraint`, or an observed-loading event. It does not promise provider-independent default-truststore behavior for null.","claim":"`init(KeyStore)` accepts a keystore “or null”; initialization precedes obtaining trust managers; the class is a factory for trust managers.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/TrustManagerFactory.html"},{"relevance":"Official Oracle JSSE provider guidance on SunJSSE trust-manager initialization, default truststore resolution, and provider-specific behavior.","limitations":"The documented default lookup concerns SunJSSE behavior and should not be generalized to arbitrary `TrustManagerFactory` providers. It does not define static-analysis semantics or require replacement of a null keystore.","claim":"For SunJSSE `PKIX`/`SunX509` trust-manager factories, the guide documents default truststore selection involving `javax.net.ssl.trustStore`, `jssecacerts`, and `cacerts`; it also documents choosing the `init` form appropriate to the factory/provider.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html"},{"relevance":"Official Java SE standard algorithm naming and mandatory-support information.","limitations":"Establishes Java SE standard support, not support in non-Java-SE platforms or every third-party provider.","claim":"Current Java SE requires support for the `PKIX` `TrustManagerFactory` algorithm; `SunX509` is not thereby made a cross-provider Java SE requirement.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html#trustmanagerfactory-algorithms"},{"relevance":"Official API documentation for persisted keystore formats and implementation-dependent storage.","limitations":"Describes `KeyStore`, rather than a migration contract for `TrustManagerFactory`; it does not establish the format of any particular existing truststore.","claim":"Keystore persistence mechanisms are unspecified and can vary; current Java SE requires `PKCS12` support.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/KeyStore.html"},{"relevance":"Historical official Java 8 API documentation for platform-version context.","limitations":"Shows the historical null-argument contract but does not supply a migration path or determine behavior of a particular provider.","claim":"Java 8 already documented `TrustManagerFactory.init(KeyStore)` as accepting a key store “or null,” and the class has existed since Java 1.4.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/javax/net/ssl/TrustManagerFactory.html"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the exact CrySL specification declarations, event patterns, order, constraints, requirements, and ensures predicates requested.","limitations":"The raw source does not identify the exact compiled/released rule artifact used by a detector invocation and does not document the analyzer's runtime matching or value-flow implementation.","claim":"The rule declares `SPEC javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory`, the listed `getInstance`, `init`, and `getTrustManagers` event patterns, `algorithm in {"PKIX", "SunX509"};`, and generated-key-store/parameter requirements; it contains no literal null or default-truststore predicate.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/TrustManagerFactory.crysl"}],"queries_performed":["Oracle Java SE 25 `javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory` API documentation","Oracle Java Secure Socket Extension (JSSE) Reference Guide trust manager and default truststore documentation","Oracle Java SE 25 Standard Names specification, TrustManagerFactory algorithms","Oracle Java SE 25 `java.security.KeyStore` API documentation","Oracle Java 8 `javax.net.ssl.TrustManagerFactory` API documentation","CROSSINGTUD Crypto-API-Rules primary source: `JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/TrustManagerFactory.crysl`"],"externally_unresolved_questions":["Which `TrustManagerFactory` provider and algorithm are actually selected at runtime, including any effect of the `ssl.TrustManagerFactory.algorithm` security property, is not established by these sources.","The values and effective behavior of `javax.net.ssl.trustStore`, `javax.net.ssl.trustStoreType`, and related truststore properties are not established.","The actual truststore source, type, contents, and whether a provider-specific `ManagerFactoryParameters` implementation is required are not established.","No official source establishes behavioral equivalence, persisted-format compatibility, or a versioned migration path between provider default truststore resolution through `init(null)` and explicitly supplied trust material.","The exact CrySL revision or compiled artifact and the detector's treatment of null, aliases, reflection, unknown values, and type resolution are not established by the raw CrySL source."]}
